{
  "image_name": "2a1fc4aa3897b6d8548fc37301691bf5.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how sex chromosome dosage and loss shape immune function and cancer outcomes. It first contrasts standard X-chromosome inactivation in female immune cells with “XCI escape,” where some genes on the usually inactive X still seem to be transcribed. This produces extra X-linked mRNA and is linked here to stronger antitumour activity compared with cells showing only the usual single active-X output.\n\nIt then broadens the idea by showing people with altered X-chromosome number. Klinefelter syndrome (XXY males) and Turner syndrome (X females) are positioned around a shared set of X-linked features, including tumour suppressors, immune genes, and hypogonadism, emphasizing that changing X dosage can affect both immunity and cancer biology.\n\nThe lower half focuses on loss of the Y chromosome. In leukocytes, ageing is associated with more mosaic loss of Y (mLOY), converting some XY blood cells into XO cells and coinciding with increased cancer risk. In tumour cells, loss of Y (LOY) is depicted as changing the tumour microenvironment: compared with a tumour retaining Y, the LOY tumour is surrounded by more immune cells and is associated with a stronger response to PD1 blockade. Overall, the image links sex chromosome composition to immune regulation and tumour behavior.",
